                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What causes airway blockage during sleep?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Airway blockage occurs when throat muscles relax during sleep, allowing soft tissues to collapse and block the airway. This can be due to enlarged tonsils, excess tissue, nasal obstruction, or anatomical factors.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How is the exact blockage location identified?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Drug-induced sleep endoscopy (DISE) allows us to visualize exactly where your airway collapses during sleep. This helps us target treatment precisely.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can blocked airway be cured without surgery?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Mild to moderate cases can often be managed with CPAP, oral appliances, or lifestyle changes. Surgery is recommended when conservative treatments fail or for anatomical causes.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the success rate of airway surgery?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Success rates vary by procedure. MMA (jaw surgery) has the highest success at 85-90%. Soft tissue surgeries depend on proper patient selection and precise technique.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How long is recovery from airway surgery?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Soft tissue surgery recovery is typically 2-3 weeks. Jaw advancement surgery requires 6-8 weeks for initial healing with full results at 3-6 months.</div></details>
